      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "Cassandra runs one async-profiler session per JVM, so only one mode runs at a time. **These panels are mutually exclusive by design.**\n\n- The **default** session starts at cluster up: `-e cpu --alloc 512k --lock 10ms`. It fills CPU, Memory Allocation, Lock Contention and Mutex Contention. Wall Clock stays empty.\n- A **wall-clock** session fills Wall Clock. Memory Allocation, Lock Contention and Mutex Contention stay empty. CPU keeps data, because Pyroscope routes runnable-thread wall samples into `process_cpu` as well.\n\nEmpty panels for the mode that is not running are expected. They do not mean the dashboard or the pipeline is broken. Run `easy-db-lab cassandra profile status` to see what each node is actually running.\n\nTo switch modes:\n\n```\neasy-db-lab cassandra profile stop\neasy-db-lab cassandra profile start -- -e wall -i 100ms\n```\n\nNever combine a CPU event and wall-clock sampling in one recording. It corrupts the CPU sample weights silently. See the [profiling guide](https://rustyrazorblade.github.io/easy-db-lab/user-guide/profiling.html).",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "One profiling mode at a time",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "grafana-pyroscope-datasource",
        "uid": "pyroscope"
      },
      "description": "Populated by a CPU session (`-e cpu`). A wall-clock session also feeds this panel, because Pyroscope routes runnable-thread wall samples into `process_cpu`.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 20,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 7
      },
      "id": 1,
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "grafana-pyroscope-datasource",
            "uid": "pyroscope"
